limitations_reported:
- Future optimizations of the task design could make the two strategies more distinguishable by increasing the proportion of trials in which behavior is consistent with one strategy but not the other;
- The imitation framework is applied to decision-making and does not address the operationalization of imitation in which specific motor actions are reproduced;
- Emulation in this task involves inferring which of three possible goals is pursued — other implementations focus on different types of social inference (inverse RL, trust learning, recursive belief inference);
- Open questions remain about how specific implementations of imitation or emulation differ mechanistically, how adaptable the arbitration framework is, and whether it can be generalized to complex real-world learning situations;
- Functional connectivity analyses needed to understand how arbitration is implemented at the network level;
- Brain stimulation methods needed to establish causal effects

